---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { isPreview } from "../lib/catalog.mjs";
---

<Base title="About the library | RuleBooks" active="about"
  ><section class="prose-page shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">ABOUT RULEBOOKS</p><h1>
      Good games deserve<br /><em>clear rules.</em>
    </h1><p class="reader-deck">
      RuleBooks is a shared library of game rules, with a path from readable
      explanations to precise, machine-readable definitions.
    </p><h2>Read first. Explore further.</h2><p>
      The ordinary question comes first: how do I play? Every guide names its
      scope, follows a readable order and keeps its sources close. Different
      editions and profiles have their own identities, so you can link to the
      rules you mean.
    </p><h2 id="editorial-status">A small, honest beginning</h2>{
      isPreview ? (
        <p>
          The first collection contains newly authored guides to Nim and
          rock-paper-scissors, plus a partial chess introduction. They are
          editorial drafts prepared with AI assistance. A complete-within-scope
          label describes the coverage of a guide; it does not mean human review
          or formal verification has occurred.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          This catalog is built from a verified publication assembly. Exact
          record revisions, attribution and acceptance evidence are available in
          its downloads. Readable completeness and executable verification
          remain separate claims.
        </p>
      )
    }<p>
      Reviewed publications retain their actual authorship and review records.
      We do not invent approvals, executable mappings or historical revisions to
      fill a page.
    </p><h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; attribution</h2><p>
      Each rule identifies its source or an explicit editorial choice. We link
      to source documents and identify their editions. Third-party reference
      material is not included in the public build. Original project content
      follows the repository’s license and source policy.
    </p><p>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/miralie-llc/rulebooks/blob/main/docs/product/repository-boundaries.md#license-scope"
        >Read the source and license policy ↗</a
      >
    </p><h2 id="contribute">Bring your games and your process</h2><p>
      A person, research tool or existing editorial team can contribute.
      Already-reviewed records can go directly into the publication contract. An
      optional draft workflow supports teams that want to preserve proposals,
      evidence and unresolved questions before acceptance.
    </p><a class="primary-link" href="https://github.com/miralie-llc/rulebooks"
      >Explore the repository ↗</a
    ><h2>Shared semantics, when they are ready</h2><p>
      RuleBooks also owns the evolving Board Game Semantic Model, or BGSM.
      Formalization will connect readable rules to shared concepts and
      executable definitions. Each game page identifies the mappings available
      for its exact rulebook revision; readable rules remain useful without
      them.
    </p>
  </section></Base
>
